On 25.04.2023, PW-1 / Plaintiff had tendered her evidence by way of affidavit exhibited as Ex.
PW1/A and relied upon the following documents:
(a) Copy of policy no. 4112-200101-16-7000100-00-000 as Mark A;
(b) Copy of ID card as Ex. PW1/2 (OSR);
(c) Salary slip of deceased Sanjay as Ex. PW1/3;
(d) Renounced Certificate of husband of the Plaintiff as Ex. PW1/4;
(e) Copy of company policy / employee benefits as Mark B;
(f) Copy of FIR as Mark C;
(g) Death certificate of deceased Sanjay as Ex. PW1/7;
(h) Copy of MLC as Mark D;
(i) Computer generated copy of surviving member certificate as Ex.
PW1/9 (Objected to by the Ld. Counsel for Defendants as to the mode of proof);
Parmila Devi Vs. Delhi Cargo Service Pvt Ltd.

Applying the principles, which have been referred to, to the facts of the present case, we summarize the following conclusions:
A. Firstly, in the MLC, in regard to the driver, the Report, inter alia, indicates that smell of alcohol (+);
B. Pertinently, the very same Report is there in regard to the co- passenger.

This is even though the Respondent had reiterated in the Rejoinder Affidavit that the driver of the vehicle had not consumed alcohol or any other intoxicating drink/drug;
Parmila Devi vs Delhi Cargo Service Pvt. Ltd on 11 December, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/90286921/
14 E.
